This project is a notebook-driven data science / ML project managed with `uv`. Follow these
conventions for every change.

## Where code goes

- Notebooks in `notebooks/` are for *exploration and narrative*: load, look, decide. They are
  numbered in the order they are meant to be read.
- Anything a second notebook would copy — a loader, a split, a metric, a training loop —
  moves into `src/{{ package_import_name }}/` and is imported back. That is the code that
  gets tests; notebooks do not.
- A notebook must run top to bottom in a fresh kernel. If it only works when cells are run
  out of order, fix the notebook rather than documenting the order.
- Never use `!pip install` in a cell: dependencies are added with `uv add`, so they are
  locked and survive a container rebuild.

## Notebooks in git

- Committed notebooks contain **no outputs**. `.githooks/pre-commit` runs `nbstripout` on the
  staged `.ipynb` files and re-stages them; do not fight it, and do not commit outputs by
  editing the JSON.
- Notebooks are linted and formatted with everything else (`ruff` has
  `extend-include = ["*.ipynb"]`): single-quoted strings, one import per line, sorted.
- Keep cells small enough that the diff of a cell is readable.

## Reproducibility

- Call `set_seed()` at the top of every notebook and pass `RANDOM_SEED` (from
  `{{ package_import_name }}.config`) to anything that samples, splits or initialises weights.
- Split data only through `data.split_train_valid_test`, so every notebook means the same
  thing by "the test set". Look at the test set once, at the end.
- Read and write paths through `{{ package_import_name }}.config` (`RAW_DATA_DIR`,
  `PROCESSED_DATA_DIR`, `MODELS_DIR`, `FIGURES_DIR`) instead of relative paths, so the same
  code works from a notebook, a script and a test.
{% if experiment_tracking != 'none' %}
- Record every training run through `{{ package_import_name }}.tracking.start_run(...)`, with
  the hyper-parameters as its params: a result that was not logged did not happen.
{% endif %}

## Data, models and figures

- `data/`, `models/` and `reports/figures/` are gitignored. Never commit a dataset, a
  checkpoint or a generated figure — regenerate them, or store them outside the repository
  (git-lfs, an object store, the Hugging Face Hub).
- Write derived data with `data.save_processed(...)` and prefer Parquet over CSV for anything
  read back by code: it keeps dtypes.
{% if include_torch %}

## Training

- The loop lives in `{{ package_import_name }}.training`, not in a notebook: notebooks choose
  the model and the hyper-parameters and call `train(...)`.
- Every run is seeded, validated once per epoch, and checkpointed into `models/`.
{% if gpu_enabled %}
- `resolve_device()` decides between CPU and CUDA — never hard-code `.cuda()`. This container
  has GPU passthrough, but the host may still not have a usable GPU.
{% else %}
- `resolve_device()` decides between CPU and CUDA — never hard-code `.cuda()`. This container
  has no GPU passthrough, so everything must also run on the CPU.
{% endif %}
- Keep `uv run python -m {{ package_import_name }}.training` working: it trains on synthetic
  data with no downloads and is the fastest way to check the loop still runs.
{% endif %}
{% if include_transformers %}
- Hub models and datasets are downloaded on first use and cached; keep the cheap
  `train_size`-limited path in the notebook working so someone can rerun it without a GPU.
{% endif %}

## Testing

- New behaviour in `src/{{ package_import_name }}/` needs a matching test in `tests/`.
- Tests must not download anything, must not need a GPU, and must not write into `data/` or
  `models/` — use `tmp_path` and monkeypatch the path constants.
